\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Transport between the two reflected forms}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let the \vocabk{connection half-algebras}{fdip-019L}
have the indicated closed site halves. On the connection
class #{\Omega} suppose the actions of #{b(x)=-x} and the
\vocabk{site reflection}{fdip-007M} #{\theta_s} are
measurable involutions given by oriented link pullback.
Let their actions preserve the probability #{\nu}. Then
#{b^*} sends #{\mathcal A_+} onto #{\mathcal A_-} and
##{B_-(b^*f,b^*g)=B_+(f,g)\qquad(f,g\in\mathcal A_+).}
Both forms are Hermitian. Positivity on either domain
therefore gives positivity on the other. The map
#{h=\theta_s b} preserves each half and satisfies
##{B_+(h^*f,h^*g)=B_+(f,g)\qquad(f,g\in\mathcal A_+).}
For this same-half equality it suffices that the spatial
reversal #{h} and #{\theta_s} act measurably on #{\Omega} and
commute, and that #{h} preserves the probability.}

\proof{
\p{The coordinate maps #{b,\theta_s} commute on vertices
and on every oriented edge. Pullback consequently commutes
on configurations, including inverses on reversed edges.
Both maps exchange the closed halves and their endpoint-wise
edge sets. A shared edge has both endpoints on time zero or
both on time #{M}, so it is a spatial boundary edge.
No time step joins the two boundary slices since #{M\ge2}.
The pullback by either map takes the sigma-algebra from one
restriction into that of the other. Each map is its own
inverse, giving equality and the assertion about half-algebras.
Their product #{h} preserves the two domains.}

\p{For #{f,g} in the positive algebra the integrand on the
left in the first identity is
#{\overline{f(b\theta_s U)}\,g(bU)}.
Commutation gives the positive integrand evaluated at
#{bU}. Substitution under the invariant probability gives
the first equality. Boundedness ensures integrability.
Substituting #{\theta_s U} in the conjugate of #{B_\pm(g,f)}
gives #{B_\pm(f,g)}, so each form is Hermitian. Onto
transport in the first identity transfers any supplied
nonnegativity of the diagonal values. With #{h} replacing
#{b}, the same calculation compares functions in the same
positive algebra, giving the last identity.}
}

\p{For the substitution, Mathlib's
\leanref{MeasureTheory.MeasurePreserving.integral_comp}
equates integrals after composition with a measure-preserving
measurable embedding. The measurable involutions here are
measurable equivalences, and the complex values form a real
normed vector space.}
